    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">July 1819</span><span class="rf-text">Bolívar's army crosses the Andes by the Páramo de Pisba and surprises the Spanish in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyaca-department">Boyacá</a> region</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1819</span><span class="rf-text">Bolívar wins a narrow victory over Barreiro at the Battle of Vargas Swamp</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1819</span><span class="rf-text">A feint at the Chicamocha River and a night countermarch let Bolívar occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunja">Tunja</a> and cut Barreiro's line to the capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1819</span><span class="rf-text">Barreiro marches round <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunja">Tunja</a> by Combita and Motavita towards Santa Fe</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1819</span><span class="rf-text">The Patriot vanguard and rearguard catch the Spanish at Casa de Teja and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyaca-department">Boyacá</a> Bridge, and the Spanish division collapses in about two hours</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1819</span><span class="rf-text">Viceroy Sámano flees Santa Fe, and Bolívar enters the city on the 10th</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1819</span><span class="rf-text">Barreiro and 38 others are executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander">Santander</a>'s orders</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">December 1819</span><span class="rf-text">Gran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> is created</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-boyaca" data-v="cf99fd8c4a" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The vanguards meet at Casa de Teja</b> The Royalist vanguard had stopped near Casa de Teja to rest. Patriot skirmishers met it, and Santander ordered the Cazadores Battalion to attack the Royalists there.</li><li><b>The Royalist vanguard falls back over the bridge</b> The Royalist vanguard crossed the bridge over the Teatinos River and took defensive positions there. Santander's full vanguard reached Casa de Teja, and he sent Bejar down the river to cross it and attack the Royalists from the rear.</li><li><b>The Royalist rearguard is cut off</b> The Royalist rearguard was still several kilometres behind, and Anzoátegui blocked the way between it and the vanguard. Outnumbered, it retreated to a small hill and formed with its guns in the centre, infantry beside them and cavalry on the wings.</li><li><b>Flank attack ordered on the Royalist rearguard</b> Anzoátegui ordered a flank attack on the Royalist rearguard. Barcelona and Bravos de Paez were to attack on the right side and the British Legion and Rifles on the left, and the two groups moved up to the Royalist line.</li><li><b>Sandes charges the Royalist guns</b> Arthur Sandes, commander of the Rifles, charged the Royalist artillery. The charge disrupted the arming of the three guns, which had been carried unassembled. Only one was put together, and it fired three shots before its carriage broke.</li><li><b>Patriot lancers drive back the Royalist dragoons</b> Bolívar ordered lancers to attack the centre of the Royalist infantry. The 3rd and 5th companies of the Granadan Dragoons charged the patriot cavalry, which turned to face them, and the dragoons retreated because the lancers' lances were longer.</li><li><b>The Royalist infantry breaks in panic</b> The Mounted Grenadiers ran towards Samaca. The charge of the two patriot cavalry squadrons caused panic among the infantry, which was formed in a column. It was ordered to charge with the bayonet but did not, and it broke.</li><li><b>Bejar fords the river behind the Royalist vanguard</b> A kilometre and a half behind Casa de Teja, Bejar's detachment managed to ford the river and approached the rear of the Royalist vanguard on the far bank.</li><li><b>Santander storms the bridge</b> When Bejar's detachment reached the Royalist vanguard, the two engaged. Santander led a bayonet charge across the bridge, and the Royalist vanguard fled, leaving its commander Colonel Juan Tolrá dead on the bridge.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar.jpg" alt="Simón Bolívar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sim%C3%B3n_Bol%C3%ADvar._Toro_Moreno,_Luis._1922,_Legislative_Palace,_La_Paz.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Luis Enrique Toro Moreno (1897-1933), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>'s Patriot Army surprised the Spanish by crossing the Andes through the Páramo de Pisba into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyaca-department">Boyacá</a> region in early July 1819, though the march had weakened his army. Viceroy Juan de Sámano ordered Colonel José María Barreiro, an artillery officer who commanded the III Division of the Expeditionary Army of Costa Firme, the Spanish force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>, to intercept and defeat Bolívar before he reached the capital, Santa Fe. The two forces met at the Battle of Vargas Swamp on 25 July, which Bolívar won narrowly.</p><p>Afterwards the Patriots camped near the battlefield and the Spanish at the town of Paipa. Both armies aimed at Santa Fe, which was defended by only some 400 Spanish troops. On 3 August the Spanish saw Bolívar's forces cross the Chicamocha River near Paipa, and at night saw him retreat to his earlier positions across it. They rested, unaware that this was a feint: Bolívar ordered a night countermarch, crossed the river again and took the alternative route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunja">Tunja</a> by the Toca road.</p><p>This put Bolívar ahead in the race to Santa Fe, reversing the earlier position. On 4 August he reached the village of Chivata at 9:00 a.m. and two hours later occupied Tunja, where he obtained 600 rifles. The city was lightly held, because its governor, Juan Lono, had left towards Paipa to bring Barreiro the supplies and reinforcements he had begged the viceroy for.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Patriot army was a combined force of Venezuelans and New Granadans together with a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of British and Irish volunteers, under the overall command of Bolívar. Brigadier General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander">Francisco de Paula Santander</a> led its Vanguard Division and Brigadier General José Antonio Anzoátegui its Rearguard Division. Units named on the day include the Cazadores Battalion under Lieutenant Colonel Joaquín París, the battalions <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and Bravos de Páez, the British Legion, the Rifles Battalion under Arthur <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sande-society">Sandes</a>.</p><p>The Spanish III Division under Barreiro included the 2nd and 3rd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numancia</a> infantry battalions with some dragoons in its vanguard under Colonel Francisco Jiménez, and the King's 1st and 2nd infantry battalions, the Mounted Grenadiers and Dragoon cavalry. On 5 August Lono gave Barreiro 12,000 cartridges and three artillery pieces, two howitzers and one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>. Barreiro, finding his line to the capital cut, marched round <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunja">Tunja</a> by night through mountain roads thought impassable in the rainy season. He left Combita at 1:00 a.m. on 6 August and reached Motavita at 11:30 a.m..</p><p>The Spanish left Motavita at 3:30 a.m. on 7 August to rejoin the Camino Real at Casa de Teja, where the Samacá road met it before it crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyaca-department">Boyacá</a> Bridge over the Teatinos River, swollen by the rains. They covered the 25 km in 7 hours 30 minutes. From the San Lázaro heights near Tunja, Bolívar saw them at about 9:00 a.m. and worked out their route. A little after 10:00 he ordered Santander and Anzoátegui to set out along the Camino Real and destroy Barreiro wherever they found him.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander">Santander</a>'s vanguard left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunja">Tunja</a> for Casa de Teja, about 16 km away, with Anzoátegui following, and the Patriots covered the distance in around four hours. Between noon and 1:00 p.m. the Spanish vanguard under Jiménez took position near Casa de Teja, stopped to eat. Shortly before 2:00 p.m. Captain Andrés Ibarra's Patriot party and the Spanish exchanged fire, and about 20 royalists chased it before returning on seeing a larger force. Santander ordered Lieutenant Colonel París to attack the Spanish at Casa de Teja.</p><p>The Spanish vanguard fell back across the Teatinos by the bridge. Santander sent Colonel Antonio Bejar down the river to cross it and take that vanguard from behind, while Anzoátegui moved to block the way between it and the Spanish rearguard, still several kilometres back. Outnumbered, the rearguard withdrew to a small hill near Casa de Teja, and Anzoátegui ordered a flank attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and Bravos de Páez on the right and the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> and the Rifles on the left.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sande-society">Sandes</a>'s charge on the Spanish guns upset their assembly, and only one fired, three shots, before it broke. Bolívar sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lance">lancers</a> against the centre, and the Granadan Dragoons' charge was turned back by Patriot cavalry with longer lances. The Mounted Grenadiers left by the Samacá road, panic spread, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonet</a> charge ordered by the Spanish was not made. Barreiro and his officers dismounted trying to hold the rout and were captured. Bejar meanwhile forded the river, and Santander led a bayonet charge across the bridge. The Spanish fled, leaving Colonel Juan Tolrá dead on it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The fighting ended shortly after 4:30 p.m., having lasted a little more than two hours. The Patriots lost 66 men, some 13 dead and 53 wounded. Barreiro's III Division suffered 100 dead, 150 wounded and 1,600 captured, and was effectively dismantled. Barreiro himself and several other Spanish commanders were among the prisoners. Most of the prisoners were Venezuelans and New Granadans and were at once taken into the Patriot army, which grew considerably and could send units to parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> not yet liberated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander">Santander</a> and Anzoátegui were promoted to General of Division.</p><p>A messenger rode 30 hours over the 100 km of bad road to Santa Fe, and the viceroy learned of the defeat at about 10:00 p.m. on 8 August. He left the capital at 9:00 a.m. on 9 August for Cartagena by way of Honda disguised as a peasant, leaving archives undestroyed and about 900,000 pesos in gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and currency. That morning Colonel Sebastián de La Calzada blew up the arsenal's powder and left with the 400 troops in the city for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popayan">Popayán</a>, and royalist sympathisers and officials also fled.</p><p>Bolívar reached Santa Fe on 10 August with a cavalry group ahead of his army and was welcomed by the inhabitants. On 18 September a victory ceremony was held in the Plaza Mayor, where twenty women of prominent families dressed in white crowned him with a laurel wreath, and Bolívar, Santander and Anzoátegui received the Cruz de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyaca-department">Boyacá</a>, a medal made for the occasion. On 11 October Santander ordered Barreiro and 38 others executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>, under the Decree of War to the Death.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known through a detailed chronology of the march and the fighting, with the hours of departure, sighting and engagement set down, and with named units and officers on each side. The casualty figures stand as one set: 13 dead and 53 wounded for the Patriots, 100 dead, 150 wounded and 1,600 captured for the Spanish. The place of the fighting, Casa de Teja beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyaca-department">Boyacá</a> Bridge on the Teatinos, is identifiable, and the site is now dotted with monuments and statues. The bridge is no longer in use but is kept as a symbol of the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>.</p><p>Details of who did what are not fully settled. Colonel Jiménez is named among the captured and Colonel Tolrá as killed on the bridge.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is considered a crucial point in the independence of the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. It ensured the success of Bolívar's campaign to liberate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> and allowed further Patriot victories at Carabobo in Venezuela, Pichincha in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, and Junín and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. The defeat of the royal forces in central New Granada weakened the rest of the royal forces in America and ended Spanish control of the provinces there when the viceroy escaped. In December 1819 New Granada and Venezuela were united in the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, called Gran Colombia. Fighting with royalist forces went on for years, and Venezuela, Peru and Ecuador became independent, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> was created, after a liberation campaign.</p><p>In Colombia 7 August is a national holiday. Every four years on that date the elected president takes the oath of office and receives the presidential sash in the Plaza de Bolívar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>. It is also Army Day, established officially in 1978 under President Alfonso López Michelsen, and on it the Colombian Army holds a large military ceremony at the site of the battle to commemorate the anniversary.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyaca-department">Boyacá</a> on 7 August 1819 a Patriot army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> caught the Spanish III Division under Barreiro as it marched for Santa Fe and in about two hours took 1,600 prisoners, for 66 casualties. The viceroy fled, and Gran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> followed in December.</em></p>

